OK, `ReadCompressedCharData` reads `len * data_size` bytes. If `data_type` is MAT_T_UINT16, `data_size = 2`, so it reads `len * 2` bytes. The buffer is `matvar->nbytes+1` bytes where `matvar->nbytes = nBytes` from the tag. But `len` is `nelems` (from `SafeMulDims`), not `nBytes / data_size`. So if `nelems` is larger than `nBytes / data_size`, it could overflow the buffer!

Wait, let me look at the call site again:

```c
case MAT_C_CHAR:
    ...
    if ( matvar->compression == MAT_COMPRESSION_ZLIB ) {
        ...
        matvar->data_type = packed_type;
        matvar->data_size = Mat_SizeOf(matvar->data_type);
        matvar->nbytes = nBytes;
    } else {
        ...
        matvar->data_type = MAT_T_UINT8;
        matvar->data_size = Mat_SizeOf(MAT_T_UINT8);
        err = SafeMul(&matvar->nbytes, nelems, matvar->data_size);
        ...
    }
    ...
    matvar->data = calloc(matvar->nbytes+1, 1);
    ...
    if ( matvar->compression == MAT_COMPRESSION_NONE ) {
        nBytes = ReadCharData(mat,(char*)matvar->data,packed_type,(int)nelems);
    }
#if defined(HAVE_ZLIB)
    else if ( matvar->compression == MAT_COMPRESSION_ZLIB ) {
        nBytes = ReadCompressedCharData(mat,matvar->internal->z,
                    (char*)matvar->data,packed_type,(int)nelems);
    }
#endif
```

In the compressed path:
- `matvar->nbytes = nBytes` (from tag)
- `matvar->data = calloc(matvar->nbytes+1, 1)` allocates `nBytes+1` bytes
- `ReadCompressedCharData(mat, z, matvar->data, packed_type, (int)nelems)` reads `nelems * data_size` bytes

If `nelems * data_size > nBytes`, this overflows the buffer!

But wait, `nelems` is calculated from `SafeMulDims(matvar, &nelems)` which multiplies all dimensions. And `nBytes` comes from the tag. So if we set dimensions such that `nelems` is large but `nBytes` is small, we get a heap buffer overflow!

